  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1248 PM MDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nNorthwest flow continues over the forecast area, as upper ridge\nsits over the western CONUS. 18Z surface analysis shows high\npressure centered over Montana this afternoon, while a frontal\nboundary sits to our south in Nebraska. Winds are currently breezy\nout of the northwest with temperatures in the upper 70s and 80s.\nCurrent radar has some light returns over south central SD and\nnortheastern WY. \n\nIsolated storms/showers will be possible this afternoon and early\nevening, however lack of any CAPE and shear should keep any storms\nsub-severe. Similar setup for Wednesday, with isolated sub-severe\nstorms possible in the lower half of the forecast area. Upper\nridge transitions over the northern and central plains on Friday,\nprompting a warming trend back into the 90s for the weekend. Next\ndisturbance to affect the region moves through on Saturday,\nhowever the better forcing currently looks like it will stay\nmostly north of our region, though still may clip northwestern\ninto central SD. \n\nBy Sunday, the upper ridge slides eastward, and southwest flow \nsets up aloft. Temperatures cool back to near normal for early \nnext week. Multiple weak disturbances will prompt near daily \nchances for isolated showers and storms in the area. Longer range \nmodels show possibly stronger wave moving through the region next \nWednesday, but will have to monitor as we move into next week.",
